{
"dur": 55.027,
"shots": [
{
"i": 1,
"start": 0.0,
"vo_start": 0.6,
"vo_end": 7.759,
"text": "In December 1960 Prime Minister Hayato Ikeda promised to double people's incomes in ten years. Critics called it a fantasy.",
"end": 8.034
},
{
"i": 2,
"start": 8.034,
"vo_start": 8.309,
"vo_end": 15.905,
"text": "The government poured money into roads, ports, railways and especially schools. Science and engineering places at universities grew fast.",
"end": 16.18
},
{
"i": 3,
"start": 16.18,
"vo_start": 16.455,
"vo_end": 23.852,
"text": "In 1950 fewer than half of Japanese children went on to high school. By the mid-1970s, more than nine in ten did.",
"end": 24.127
},
{
"i": 4,
"start": 24.127,
"vo_start": 24.402,
"vo_end": 32.358,
"text": "Young people from the countryside rode special trains to city factories. Companies were so short of workers that they called them golden eggs.",
"end": 32.633
},
{
"i": 5,
"start": 32.633,
"vo_start": 32.908,
"vo_end": 39.666,
"text": "But where did the money for all of this come from? Japan had no oil and no colonies. It came from families.",
"end": 39.941
},
{
"i": 6,
"start": 39.941,
"vo_start": 40.216,
"vo_end": 49.932,
"text": "Japanese households saved more of their income than almost any rich country, over twenty percent by the mid-1970s. Banks and post offices lent that money to the factories.",
"end": 50.207
},
{
"i": 7,
"start": 50.207,
"vo_start": 50.482,
"vo_end": 54.077,
"text": "The income-doubling plan was reached in about seven years, not ten.",
"end": 55.027
}
],
